WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.340
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:09.210
Neural network-based learning methods are
capable of wondrous things these days.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:14.320
They can do classification, which means that
they look at an image, and the output is a

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:20.910
decision, whether we see a dog or a cat, or
a sentence that describes an image.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:26.130
In the case of DeepMind’s AI playing Atari
games, the input is the video footage of the

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:31.630
game, and the output is an action that decides
what we do with our character next.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:39.201
In OpenAI’s amazing Jukebox paper, the input
was a style of someone, a music genre, and

00:00:39.201 --> 00:00:45.850
lyrics, and the output was a waveform, or
in other words, a song we can listen to.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:53.360
But, a few hundred episodes ago we covered
a paper from 2015, where scientists at DeepMind

00:00:53.360 --> 00:01:00.230
asked the question, what if we would get these
neural networks to output not sentences, decisions,

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:06.799
waveforms or any of that sort… what if the
output would be a computer program?

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:09.760
Can we teach a neural network programming?

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:15.200
I was convinced that the answer is no, until
I saw these results.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.330
So what is happening here?

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:22.570
The input is a scratch pad where we are performing
multi-digit addition in front of the curious

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:24.760
eyes of the neural network.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:29.979
And if it has looked for long enough, it was
indeed able to produce a computer program

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:33.689
that could, eventually, perform addition.

00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:39.100
It could also perform sorting, and would even
be able to rotate the images of these cars

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:40.960
into a target position.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.939
It was called a neural programmer-interpreter,
and of course, it was slow and a bit inefficient,

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:52.970
but no matter, because it could finally make
something previously impossible possible.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:54.840
That is an amazing leap.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.270
So, why are we talking about this work from
2015?

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:03.689
Well, apart from the fact that there are many
amazing works that are timeless, and this

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:08.779
is one of them, in this series, I always say,
two more papers down the line, and it will

00:02:08.779 --> 00:02:10.910
be improved significantly.

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:14.290
So here is the Two Minute Papers Moment of
Truth.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:17.260
How has this area improved with this followup
work?

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:22.900
Let’s have a look at this paper from scientists
at NVIDIA that implements a similar concept

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:25.489
for computer games.

00:02:25.489 --> 00:02:28.090
So how is that even possible?

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:33.939
Normally, if we wish to write a computer game,
we first, envision the game in our mind, then,

00:02:33.939 --> 00:02:36.050
we sit down and do the programming.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:39.239
But this new paper does this completely differently.

00:02:39.239 --> 00:02:44.919
Now, hold on to your papers, because this
is a neural network-based method, that first,

00:02:44.919 --> 00:02:50.000
looks at someone playing the game, and then,
it is able to implement the game so that it

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:56.170
not only looks like it, but it also behaves
the same way to our keypresses.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:57.919
You see it at work here.

00:02:57.919 --> 00:03:02.799
Yes, this means that we can even play with
it and it learns the internal rules of the

00:03:02.799 --> 00:03:07.249
game and the graphics, just by looking at
some gameplay.

00:03:07.249 --> 00:03:12.549
Note that the key part here is that we are
not doing any programming by hand - the entirety

00:03:12.549 --> 00:03:14.819
of the program is written by the AI.

00:03:14.819 --> 00:03:19.719
We don’t need access to the source code
or the internal workings of the game, as long

00:03:19.719 --> 00:03:22.779
as we can just look at it, it can learn the
rules.

00:03:22.779 --> 00:03:28.400
Everything truly behaves as expected, we can
even pick up the capsule and eat the ghosts

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.530
as well.

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:34.489
This sounds like science fiction, and we are
not nearly done yet!

00:03:34.489 --> 00:03:36.229
There are additional goodies.

00:03:36.229 --> 00:03:41.689
It has memory and uses it consistently, or,
in other words, things don’t just happen

00:03:41.689 --> 00:03:42.959
arbitrarily.

00:03:42.959 --> 00:03:47.769
If we return to a state of the game that we
visited before, it will remember to present

00:03:47.769 --> 00:03:50.489
us with very similar information.

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:55.900
It also has an understanding of foreground
and background, dynamic and static objects

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:02.840
as well, so we can experiment with replacing
these parts, thereby reskinning our games.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:07.699
It still needs quite a bit of data to perform
all this as it has looked at approximately

00:04:07.699 --> 00:04:14.749
120 hours of footage of the game being played,
however, now, something is possible that was

00:04:14.749 --> 00:04:16.910
previously impossible.

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:21.910
And of course, two more papers down the line,
this will be improved significantly I am sure.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:27.530
I think this work is going to be one of those
important milestones that remind us that many

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:33.080
of the things that we had handcrafted methods
for will, over time, be replaced with these

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:34.310
learning algorithms.

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:40.240
They already know the physics of fluids, or
in other words, they are already capable of

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.639
looking at videos of these simulations and
learn the underlying physical laws, and they

00:04:45.639 --> 00:04:50.800
can demonstrate having learned general knowledge
of the rules by being able to continue these

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:55.509
simulations, even if we change the scene around
quite a bit.

00:04:55.509 --> 00:05:00.470
In light transport research, we also have
decades of progress in simulating how rays

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:05.139
of light interact with scenes and we can create
these beautiful images.

00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:10.389
Parts of these algorithms, for instance, noise
filtering are already taken over by AI-based

00:05:10.389 --> 00:05:15.389
techniques, and I can’t help but feel that
a bigger tidal wave is coming.

00:05:15.389 --> 00:05:21.009
This tidal-wave will be an entirely AI-driven
technique that will write the code for the

00:05:21.009 --> 00:05:23.210
entirety of the system.

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:27.970
Sure the first ones will be limited, for instance,
this is a neural renderer from one of our

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:33.259
papers that is limited to this scene and lighting
setup, but you know the saying, two more papers

00:05:33.259 --> 00:05:36.449
down the line, and it will be an order of
magnitude better.

00:05:36.449 --> 00:05:41.330
I can’t wait to tell all about it to you
with a video when this happens.

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:46.000
Make sure to subscribe and hit the bell icon
to not miss any followup works.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.840
Goodness, I love my job.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:06:34.370
What a time to 
be alive!

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:42.249
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

